INT. FIELD HOSPITAL TENT - FLOOD RELIEF CAMP - NIGHT

Canvas walls breathing in the wind. Cots in rows. Hanging bulbs on extension cords. The smell of bleach and wet earth.

Five people around a single cot. On it, the PATIENT, a man in his 60s, gray-faced, breathing shallowly. Nobody says his name.

DR. ADAEZE OKONKWO-REYES (40s, scrubs, sleeves rolled, sweat at her temples) checks a monitor that blinks erratically. NURSE PETRA LINDBERG-AZIZ (30s, steady, a pen behind each ear) squeezes a bag valve. MEDIC SAMUEL TORRES-WU (20s, wet boots) holds a flashlight. DR. HENRI DUBOIS-KAMARA (50s, retired surgeon, borrowed lab coat) stands at the foot with his arms folded. The man's DAUGHTER, JOY BANERJEE-HALL (30s, a muddy raincoat), grips the cot rail.

ADAEZE
(to the room)
Okay. Everyone. Pressure's dropping. Petra, push the fluids. Samuel, hold the light steady. Henri...

HENRI
I'm observing.

ADAEZE
I know. Observe harder.

Petra changes the bag. Samuel moves the light. The man's chest rises and falls.

JOY
(to Adaeze)
Is he going to be all right?

ADAEZE
We're doing everything we can.

JOY
That's not what I asked.

ADAEZE
I know.

She turns back to the monitor. A beep. Another. A flat trailing note.

PETRA
Doctor.

ADAEZE
I see it.

SAMUEL
(muttering)
Come on, come on, come on.

Samuel angles the light, then glances at Joy and looks away.

HENRI
Adaeze. The line is infiltrated.

ADAEZE
What?

HENRI
The IV. It's swelling at the wrist.

She looks. He's right. Her jaw tightens. She pulls the line herself, fast, and the man gasps.

JOY
What are you doing?

ADAEZE
Resetting it.

JOY
You're hurting him.

ADAEZE
(sharp)
Then let me work.

Joy flinches. Adaeze catches herself. She closes her eyes for a half second, then opens them.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
(softer)
Petra. Another line. The other arm.

PETRA
Already done.

Petra holds out the needle, a bead of blood on her thumb. Adaeze takes it. Her hands, steady a moment ago, tremble now. She sets the needle. Draws breath. Pushes.

The monitor catches. Beeps. A ragged line climbs.

SAMUEL
We've got him.

HENRI
We have a rhythm. Not a pulse.

ADAEZE
(to Henri)
Come here.

He comes. She hands him the bag valve without a word. He takes it, stunned. Then he squeezes, once, twice, and glances across the cot at her.

HENRI
(quiet)
I forgot how this feels.

ADAEZE
Don't remember now. Squeeze.

Joy's knuckles are white on the rail. Samuel puts a hand on her shoulder. Joy does not shake it off.

The man's eyelids flutter. His mouth moves. Joy leans in, hair dripping onto his cheek.

JOY
Dad? Dad, it's me.

Nothing. Then a breath, deeper than the others, a long ragged pull. Everyone stops moving.

Outside, the wind drops. For the first time in hours, the tent hangs still.
